{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact LOG-1 head 37e98a9b56f9472680a4f8bee9a41fd076bd357f on builder/handle-log-d1-20261112, all 7 artifacts, for request e79ccfaf under promise 80a0c32f and review request 76ea606e. The head does what e79ccfaf asks, with the cut tail that ruling ecdcd5c0 adds. `ScopeHandle.log` returns a checked page that says whether a fold may use it. The transport, the scope, the wire and the kernel do not change.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- `ScopeHandle.log` (`packages/client/src/handle.ts`):\n  - It reads the summary once and keeps the scope reference and definition.\n  - It refuses a minimum of another scope before the read and a page below the minimum after it.\n  - It rejects a page of another scope or definition.\n  - For each row, it checks that the bytes hash to the row's hash under the entry domain. It checks that the parsed entry holds the row's seq and this scope's reference, and that the seqs rise from the cursor and stay at or below the head.\n  - A refusal, `archived` included, passes through unchanged.\n- Coverage is `whole` only when both conditions of ecdcd5c0 hold:\n  - The handle's own reader is a `Session ` text.\n  - The seqs run without a gap from the cursor to `next`, or to the head when the page has no `next`.\n  - A reader of any other kind, a cursor that is not a number, and a `next` that is not a number all give `filtered`.\n  - The check uses the reader the caller gave the handle, not the transport's. A handle with a null reader over `signedReads` therefore reads `filtered`, as the cut-tail test shows.\n- The scope keeps every row for a member session and for a register session, as 5af8b09c found and ecdcd5c0 accepts. A caller can give any `Session ` text, but the scope authenticates it, and a refused session returns a refusal, not a page.\n- `packages/client/README.md` gains \"Folding a scope's journal\". It states the checks, when a page is whole, which reads give whole pages, and that a `filtered` page must not feed a replica.\n- `apps/room/page/assets.ts` is the Page bundle rebuilt with the new method.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. Main e7db3872 is an ancestor, and the pushed branch equals the head. The builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the 7 paths that the 7 artifacts cover, all at the head. The primary artifact is neither stale nor retired.\n- Cheap checks. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings. The commit-message check passes: 3 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean.\n- Tests, on my own clone of the head. `packages/client/test/handle-log.test.ts` passes 5 of 5. I read the scope witness `packages/scope/test/handle-log.test.ts` and did not run it: it runs in workerd, and the gate covers it. It folds the four whole pages of a real ten-entry scope with `applyEntry` and matches the scope's own state digest.\n- Evidence. All 50 files that the manifest lists match their SHA-256 values in `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence`. The gate at b54bb9f8 wrote exit 0 and \"gate passed\", and its counts agree with END 69fa6fe5. Its tree equals the manifest's `checkpointTree` and the tree of 6fea47d5. From b54bb9f8 to the head only the note and the manifest change. The two stopped starts, 2ab52bee and 33cdf309, ended with exit 130, and the note claims nothing for them.\n- CI. Of the changed paths, only `apps/room/page/assets.ts` matches the Page workflow's `paths` filter, and none matches the Card workflow's filter. GitHub reports Page run 43366646400 as a push event, attempt 1, completed and successful, at 6fea47d5, which has the gated tree.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The handle owns the checks, because only the handle knows which reader it used. The scope keeps one rule for which rows a reader sees, and the client reports that rule's effect without changing it.\n\nSecurity: no finding. A signed page can never count as whole, so the cut tail cannot feed a replica. One note for a later head. `parseStrict` accepts whitespace, so a row's bytes need not be canonical. The handle then checks the hash of those bytes, which can differ from `entryHash` of the parsed entry. `applyEntry` checks each entry's `prev` against the fold's head, so a fold of such a page fails at the next entry rather than drifting silently. Comparing `canonicalize(entry)` with `row.bytes` in the handle would refuse the page at once. The note's phrase \"parse as canonical values\" should then say canonical bytes.\n\nSimplification: no required change. The cursor test `/^(0|[1-9][0-9]{0,15})$/` appears twice in `log`. One small helper that turns a cursor into a number or null would serve both.\n\nThis approval authorizes no landing or deployment. The planner decides the landing and deploys.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c161d8f254367fa4b5ddb0b9f59dfdb81927d5fa","binding":"assigned","head":"37e98a9b56f9472680a4f8bee9a41fd076bd357f","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fd63aad7f2cb673ca7a6ba407d97e9f2fe1cdd70\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:80a0c32f31df2b556cebd4a294be06b4068f55ec\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dc56a29df0c187af35d40d7583a381994e29738f","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}